{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_7","type":"agent_message","text":"{\"submission\":\"update.json\",\"summary\":\"Added the last item the open gap named for the event set: node restarts. A restart event now picks a node, persists what an application is documented to persist - the node's hard state through SetHardState and its applied ConfState into the storage snapshot metadata - then rebuilds the instance with newRaft over that storage with Config.Applied set to the node's applied index and re-registers it with the network; both supplied pieces follow from the knowledge established earlier, since newRaft reads configuration only from Storage.InitialState's ConfState and MemoryStorage never tracks it, and a restart without hard state would silently lose term and vote. The sweep also keeps joint transitions (half the configuration proposals use JointImplicit, so the leave proposals come from the target's own AutoLeave path), a real joining participant as node 4, and committed conf changes applied through applyConfChange. Draft-side this revision reports seeds=60, steps_per_seed=60, elections=466, ordinary proposals=452, conf_change_proposals=249, restarts=471, violations=0, so the four invariants - including per-node committed-index monotonicity, the one a restart could most plausibly break if a node returned with a lower commit index - still show no witness; because that is construction feedback only, a fresh isolated execution of these bytes is requested with the changed event set as the revision reason. Still unbuilt and therefore still open in gap 984747de5ed94b4ea8f6977b2a92808f-gap-2: the liveness witness recording whether committed-but-unapplied conf changes can leave a configuration without an electable voter, and demotion of an existing serving voter rather than the joining node.\"}"}}
